=== REASONING ===

ADR-015 (AI-Off Contract) requires that ai_mode='off' produce a system
indistinguishable, on the wire and in the DOM, from a build with no AI
code at all. The slice satisfies this in three layers:

  1. Backend (I6 — 404 routes in off mode): the new /api/v1/ai/chatbot
     handler is mounted under ai.GuardedHandler('chatbot-llm', ...).
     The guard returns http.StatusNotFound *before* the handler runs
     when settings.ai_mode='off' (see internal/ai/guard/guard.go).
     TestChatbotAIOffUsesBaselineAndAiRoute404 in
     internal/api/ai_chatbot_handler_test.go pins this with a stub
     guard.Settings whose Mode()='off' returns 404 even when the
     per-feature flag chatbot-llm=true. The test also asserts the
     404 body does NOT mention "GUARD_BYPASSED", "chatbot-llm",
     "feature", "strategy", or "provider" — i.e., the guard does
     not leak the existence of the AI surface.

  2. Frontend (I5 — hidden UI in off mode + I7 — per-feature opt-in):
     the visible AI surface is AIChatbotIndicator =
     withAiFeature('chatbot-llm', InnerIndicator). useAiEnabled
     returns false when EITHER mode='off' OR the per-feature toggle
     is false, and withAiFeature returns null in either case. The
     new ChatbotAIOff.test.tsx contains three cases that prove this
     gate is symmetric:
       - mode='off' + toggle=true  → null (mode wins)
       - mode='cloud' + toggle=false → null (toggle wins)
       - mode='cloud' + toggle=true  → renders, with the expected
         data-testid='ai-feature-chatbot-llm-root' attribute. This
         "positive control" defeats the trivial-pass failure mode
         (where both negative assertions would pass even if the
         indicator were permanently hidden).

  3. Baseline coexistence (I3 — baseline intact): the existing
     POST /chatbot route is wholly unchanged. It is mounted
     unconditionally in router.go (~line 1354) with no guard
     wrapper, and ChatbotPage continues to issue baseline calls
     through useSendChatMessage. The slice's only header change to
     ChatbotPage is the conditional <AIChatbotIndicator /> which is
     null in off mode. The new internal/api/chatbot_responder.go
     formalises the seam: ChatResponder is an interface with one
     method Respond(ctx, msg) (string, error); BaselineResponder
     wraps *ChatbotHandler and delegates to processQuery, exposing
     the heuristic chatbot to future code (e.g., a ResponderRouter)
     without changing today's behaviour.

P1 (provider port) — the AI handler resolves provider.Streamer through
provider.RegistryFromContext(ctx). The handler does NOT import any
specific provider package; the provider chain is entirely assembled at
router construction time and pushed via context.

P5 (decorator chain) — the slice does NOT add or skip any decorator;
the locked order from F8/F9 is honoured: WithRedaction(PolicyFromContext)
→ WithAudit(aiAuditWriter) → WithTrace. F9's WithRateLimit / WithCostCap
are intentionally NOT wired here; F9's own log records that wiring is
deferred to a later "router-wiring slice".

P6 (guard + withAiFeature) — both forms used. Backend: ai.GuardedHandler
in mountAIRoutes. Frontend: withAiFeature HOC with the same featureID
('chatbot-llm') so the guard, the feature toggle, and the wrapper all
agree on the same registry key.

P9 (registry) — UITestIDs renamed from "ai-feature-chatbot-llm" to the
prompt-required "ai-feature-chatbot-llm-root" (registry.go L116). The
generated TS mirror web/src/ai/features.ts was regenerated via
`go run ./tools/aigen` so the registries stay in sync (the mismatch
detector tools/aivet would otherwise BLOCK on the divergence).

P8 (eval goldens) — strategy ships goldens.yaml with 5 deterministic
goldens (range_question, tool_call_battery, tool_call_then_answer,
refusal, ambiguous), exceeding the prompt's "≥3" floor. Tools list
in the YAML was reconciled with the strategy whitelist; mutating_tools
is explicitly [] (chatbot is read-only).

P10 (baseline coexistence) — see point 3 above. The selection mechanism
is "ChatResponder is selected at construction by ai_mode plus feature
flag; baseline route remains unconditional", as declared in the prompt's
"Baseline coexistence (P10)" section.

Rejected alternatives:
  - Using RegisterStrategy(eval.Strategy) — rejected because the eval
    harness uses GenericStrategy synthesized from FeatureSpec when no
    real Strategy is registered. The 5 goldens pass without it; adding
    a custom strategy provider adds wiring without functional benefit.
  - Adding chatbot.llm.* keys to ar.json/he.json — rejected because
    those locales are placeholders; F9 set the precedent of adding to
    en.json only and relying on i18next.fallbackLng. Translators will
    add the keys when they translate the rest of the locale.
  - Wiring F9 decorators (WithRateLimit, WithCostCap) — rejected
    because F9's own log defers router wiring; doing it here would
    overstep the slice's scope.
  - Permanently disabling the F0 stub — rejected because tests construct
    the router without the AI handler; the stub remains as the "no AI
    handler wired" fallback, returning the same 501 it always did.

